# Bulk corpus is regenerable from `python -m src.data` — never committed (§4.3).
data/*.txt
data/*.bin
data/*.json
# Packed SFT tensors: 74 MB, and fully determined by sft_train.json + the
# tokenizer + context_len, so `python -m src.sft_data build` regenerates them
# byte-for-byte. The counts that matter are in results/sft_budget_census.json.
data/*.npy
!data/.gitkeep

# ...but these three are small and are the audit trail, so they ship:
#   tokenizer.json      part of the model — a checkpoint without it is unusable
#   tokenizer_meta.json vocab size, separator id, shard token counts
#   manifest.json       dataset sha, license, repair counts, shard hashes;
#                       lets a reader verify our numbers without a 17-min build
!data/tokenizer.json
!data/tokenizer_meta.json
!data/manifest.json

# Weights. The tiny final checkpoint is force-added at release time.
checkpoints/*.pt
!checkpoints/.gitkeep

# Results are committed (they are the artifact) except bulky intermediates.
results/**/*.png.tmp

# Environment
.venv/
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# Local HF cache if it ever lands inside the repo
.cache/

# The HF Space is BUILT from the repo, never hand-maintained.
# `python -m scripts.build_space` regenerates it and asserts byte-identity to
# source, so the build output is derived and not committed. The authored
# Space-specific files live in space/ and ARE committed.
build/

# Credentials. Belt-and-braces only: the real deploy token lives OUTSIDE this
# repo at ~/.nlemon-deploy.env, because a token inside a repo is one bad glob
# away from being published. These patterns catch a token that lands here by
# accident anyway.
.env
.env.*
*.env
# Deliberately NOT a broad `*token*` rule: it matches data/tokenizer.json and
# src/tokenizer.py, which must ship. Over-broad ignore rules that need three
# negations to undo are how a required file goes missing from a build.
